text.en:
Ensure architectural systems connect to and depend upon structural construction without dimensional conflicts, unsupported conditions, improper penetrations or compromised architectural performance.
Typical interfaces include:
wall framing to structure;
masonry to structure;
curtain/cladding supports;
roof/parapet interfaces;
door openings;
slab edges;
stairs;
architectural embeds;
ceilings;
fire-rated wall terminations.

Ensure mechanical systems are supported and routed without compromising structural elements, while structural construction incorporates all required openings, embeds, sleeves, pads and supports.
Typical interfaces:
pipe/duct penetrations;
equipment bases;
hangers;
sleeves;
openings;
roof curbs;
housekeeping pads;
structural supports for equipment;
vibration-isolation interfaces.

Ensure mechanical systems cross or connect to the building envelope without compromising water, air, vapour or thermal performance.
Examples:
exterior duct penetration;
intake/exhaust louvres;
roof pipes;
rooftop equipment;
wall-mounted mechanical equipment;
drains;
exhaust terminations.

Ensure electrical services penetrate or mount to the envelope without compromising air, water, vapour and thermal continuity.
Examples:
conduits;
cable trays;
exterior equipment;
wall boxes;
roof penetrations;
exterior lighting;
grounding interfaces.

This is one of the most common coordination zones.
Ensure MEP installations integrate into architectural walls, ceilings, floors, doors and finishes without compromising appearance, fire/acoustic performance, access or functionality.
Examples:
diffusers in ceilings;
lights;
sprinklers;
detectors;
electrical boxes;
pipe penetrations;
access panels;
floor drains;
wall-mounted equipment.

Ensure every cast-in item required by another discipline is correctly identified, coordinated, positioned and secured before concrete permanently conceals it.
Examples:
sleeves;
anchor bolts;
inserts;
grounding;
conduits;
plates;
drains;
embedded supports.
